<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huave</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ikoots</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huave, who call themselves Ikoots, are an Indigenous people of the Pacific coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, living around coastal lagoons where fishing is central to their way of life. Their language is an isolate, unrelated to its neighbours. In the late fifteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> pushed them out of much of the isthmus, and today they live mainly in towns such as San Mateo del Mar.</p></div>
